Conducting high-throughput experiments on genome-scale is labor-intensive and costly. As such, reliable alternatives are urgently needed to streamline experimental design and analysis. Here, we develop Neo-SIM - a data-driven, iterative framework for unbiased analysis of genome-scale metabolic networks using Drosophila fruit flies. Inspired by evolutionary robotics, we provide a general yet flexible framework to automatically build protein-level pathways and phenotypes from highly trained yet diverse Drosophila genomes. We develop an interactive graphical user interface (GUI) and integrated plug-in system (PLIN) for metabolic and developmental genome analysis. Using Neo-SIM, we demonstrate that a broad range of biologists can analyze high-throughput metabolic networks in Drosophila and study phenotypic changes resulting from tissue lineage rearrangements or intrinsic variation. We additionally show that it can be extended to systems with more complex phenotypes, notably performing energy analyses on meta-data from an external feed-forward controller system. As an additional contribution, we model the experimental constraints of meta-analysis and analyze their influence on gene expression changes. We further apply Neo-SIM to study various types of tissue-level phenotypic variations. Neo-SIM analyses all tissues of the fruit fly, providing functional, evolutionary and chemical explanations for phenotypic variability. We observe an evolutionarily conserved shift from oxidative to aerobic metabolism during Drosophila diversification, consistent with independently acquired genes enabling the aerobic mitochondrial terminal oxidase complex. We demonstrate that Neo-SIM can accommodate data-driven non-linear optimization problems and infer metabolic bottlenecks and potential bottlenecks using a time-reversible likelihood. Neo-SIM outperforms prior approaches in performance, space and parameter selections by up to 5 orders of magnitude.

